##yabai on/off
# yabai's own --start-service/--stop-service are hardcoded to the label
# com.koekeishiya.yabai, which is not what runs here: this machine loads yabai
# from ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.asmvik.yabai.plist. So both flags were no-ops
# -- bootout could not find the job, and --start-service would have installed a
# second, competing agent. Drive the real label instead.
#
# bootout, not `launchctl kill`: KeepAlive restarts yabai on a crash or a
# non-zero exit, so only unloading the job actually stops it.
#
# Exactly one of bootstrap/kickstart, never both. This plist sets RunAtLoad, so
# bootstrap already starts yabai -- chaining a kickstart onto it (the dance
# bin/qs-open does, for a plist that does not) races a second instance, which
# exits because the first holds the mach port, and launchd then sees the job's
# process gone with a clean status and KeepAlive's SuccessfulExit=false leaves
# it dead. So: loaded -> kickstart, not loaded -> bootstrap.
ctrl + alt + cmd - o : launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.asmvik.yabai
ctrl + alt + cmd - n : launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/com.asmvik.yabai >/dev/null 2>&1 && launchctl kickstart gui/$(id -u)/com.asmvik.yabai || launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.asmvik.yabai.plist


## toggle window fullscreen zoom
ctrl + alt + cmd - f : yabai -m window --toggle zoom-fullscreen

#locking the computer (native display sleep -- password/Touch ID required to
#wake, since "require password immediately after sleep" is on; the classic
#CGSession -suspend trick no longer exists on this macOS version)
ctrl + alt + cmd - l : /usr/bin/pmset displaysleepnow

